            "script": "<p><span class=\"ts\">(14:41 - 15:47)</span> Whereas today we use plaster and paint or wallpaper, the Iron Age people had a mixture of clay, cow dung and straw, which they daubed all over the inside and outside to give the house a smooth finish. You will hear two business students called Emma and Nick preparing for their presentation on the topic of how jobs might change in the future. First you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:18 - 16:37)</span> Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24. OK Nick, the title of our presentation is How Jobs Might Change in the Future. Shall we look at the research we've done so far? I was just watching an interview with Alex Coburn.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:38 - 16:55)</span> Go on. He talked about a company with a call centre for dealing with complaints. Instead of following a script with a list of questions they had to ask, the call centre employees were told they could deal with complaints however they wanted, which went down well with them.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:55 - 17:02)</span> That's not a common approach these days. But apparently it worked. <b data-q=\"21\">Customers were happier with the new methods.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:03 - 17:09)</span> Really? That's certainly not the result you'd expect. We'll definitely have to use that example. Maybe.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:10 - 17:31)</span> What about Gerry Kaplan's report? He argues there'll be plenty of jobs not suitable for machines. Like, a machine might be able to diagnose your disease, but patients will always want to talk face to face with their doctor. <b data-q=\"22\">I suppose it's something to do with the fact that a human can empathise, but a machine can't.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:31 - 17:43)</span> Right. Now, there's that article called The Human Cloud. It describes the growing numbers of people being employed as contractors to do online tasks at a distance.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:44 - 17:57)</span> Well, that could be good for the companies. They can choose the most expert people available, and they get cheap labour and no costs except wages. But for the workers, it may mean less money.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:58 - 18:12)</span> <b data-q=\"23\">On the other hand, I love the idea of working from home in a time that suits.</b> It's certainly controversial. Another article was the one looking at how employers will use technology to record what their employees are doing.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:12 - 18:25)</span> So awful. Cameras in the workplace checking on where you are, software that tells your boss who you've been emailing. When we talk about this, I want to make clear how this affects people's personal privacy.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:25 - 18:38)</span> Did you read how some companies are tracking the health of their employees? Surely workers won't accept that. <b data-q=\"24\">Yes, I'd be very surprised if this became common. Too many employees would refuse.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:41 - 19:25)</span> Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30. Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30. OK.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(19:26 - 19:34)</span> There's some aspects of the presentation we still need to think about. Yes, there are a few issues. Let's go through them one by one.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(19:34 - 19:42)</span> OK. What about the introduction then? Well, we've sorted out the content. Your editing has helped.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(19:42 - 19:50)</span> <b data-q=\"25\">But don't you think you ought to deliver it rather than me? It's mainly your work.</b> OK, so let's do it that way then. Good.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(19:50 - 20:10)</span> What about the researcher profiles? Kathy Bryson and Alex Coburn's backgrounds? I doubt we'll have time to look at them properly during the presentation. <b data-q=\"26\">I reckon we should just attach that bit to the written report we've given to the tutor. So, cut it?</b> Let's rehearse the whole presentation and see if it's necessary.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(20:11 - 20:25)</span> What about the section on automation? Aren't you happy with it? I'm thinking <b data-q=\"27\">I haven't really given equal attention to the pros and cons. I should start again.</b> Well, let's have a bit more of a think about it.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(20:25 - 20:39)</span> You don't want to rush into a big job like that. Another thing is the section on workplace structure. I think we've got some good points, but... <b data-q=\"28\">We're only using one particular company as a case study.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(20:39 - 20:49)</span> We need to source some other situations as supporting material. So perhaps we could both do a search and compare notes. And choose the most interesting cases.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(20:49 - 21:14)</span> And the section on tracking technology. Are we happy with that? It would definitely benefit from some photos. Hang on. <b data-q=\"29\">There's no point using images just for decoration. We need to think about whether they'd serve any real purpose.</b> Fair enough. Let's make a decision on that at a later stage. And that interview we did with the company manager. Yes, we were lucky she gave us her time.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(21:14 - 21:26)</span> But I just want to be sure we wrote everything down accurately. I wouldn't like to misrepresent her views. Maybe I should type up that section and send it to her.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(21:26 - 21:31)</span> <b data-q=\"30\">So she can confirm whether or not it's correct.</b> Yes, that's a good idea. Go ahead.</p>"
