            "script": "<p>Part 3. You will hear a mature student on a teacher training programme discussing a new course with his college lecturer. First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 23 on page 6. Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 23.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(15:10 - 15:12)</span> Dr. Williams. Come in. Thanks.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(15:12 - 15:27)</span> How can I help you? Well, I'm interested in doing the course on action research. Sure. What would you like to know? Well, first of all, I understand it's about ways of researching your own practise in your professional role so as to improve it.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(15:28 - 15:48)</span> That's right, and I'm afraid I must emphasise at the outset that it's only open to working teachers. Well, I've been one for eight years, so... That's plenty, and... And can I ask, will you be teaching out? Yes, I've done so for many years, but the course leader will be one of our new lecturers, Alan Cumner. Oh.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(15:49 - 16:10)</span> He's just written a very interesting book on action research, which you should get hold of. <b data-q=\"21\">The title is Professional Learning.</b> Right. I think one of the other teachers in my school mentioned it. Okay. Can I ask you what will be covered on the course? Well, the obvious aim is to develop skills in using a variety of research techniques.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:11 - 16:19)</span> Yes, of course. I'll ask you about those in more detail later. And then there's something that's crucial to action research, and <b data-q=\"22\">that's the skill of researching in a team.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:20 - 16:33)</span> Yes. I can say that it is very important. And what about after you've done the research? Do you do something on dissemination of findings? Well, the last major objective is similar to that, but not quite the same.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(16:33 - 17:23)</span> <b data-q=\"23\">It's on presenting results as part of the wider collaborative culture.</b> Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 24 to 30 on page 7. Now listen and answer questions 24 to 30. Great. Well, that's very helpful. I can ask you about the different components. Well, the course is organised around the data-gathering techniques, and we divide them into two main types, observational and non-observational techniques.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:24 - 17:34)</span> And between each session, we ask you to try out the technique for yourself in a variety of settings. The initial sessions are on using observation checklists. Oh, yes.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:35 - 17:48)</span> And we ask you to try some out in a school <b data-q=\"24\">so you can look at an element of a lesson, such as pupil behaviour</b>, so you can immediately apply what you've learnt to a real situation in the classroom. Right. <b data-q=\"25\">Somebody told me we have to keep a diary.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:49 - 17:57)</span> Yes, that's right. We ask you to keep one as a research tool. And what's the point of that exactly? Well, it really enhances your capacity for reflection.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(17:58 - 18:22)</span> Oh, that sounds interesting. And then <b data-q=\"26\">we've just added another observational technique to the course, which is video recording.</b> I have tried that already with colleagues. I have to say, with only limited success. Yes, that's been our experience too, especially at the beginning. So we decided to get you to <b data-q=\"27\">try this technique out with your fellow students in classroom simulation</b>, which I think you'll agree.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:23 - 18:34)</span> That has fewer problems, yes. And then we have sessions examining what we call non-observational techniques. And rather controversially, <b data-q=\"28\">we start with ways of scrutinising test results.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:35 - 18:41)</span> Oh, right. Yeah, I'm familiar with that. Yes, but we think these can yield some very valuable insights.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:42 - 18:50)</span> We ask you to bring along statistics relating to your own students for this. Oh, that should be easy. I have to say I quite enjoy that kind of analysis.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(18:51 - 19:14)</span> Yes. And then we look at using questionnaires. Oh, yes. I've done one or two projects involving those. I used one local teacher's resource group, but I've never felt that I've used them as effectively as I should have done so. Well, that is a common comment from our participants, and we thought we'd open things up a bit this year with a different approach and <b data-q=\"29\">use the Internet in order to identify respondents.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(19:15 - 19:48)</span> Obviously, we'd explain more about how that will work closer to the time. Great. Look forward to that. <b data-q=\"30\">And will there be anything on interviews? That's really the last major section</b>, and we ask you to choose your own respondents for that as part of a final project. Great. Well, that's been really helpful, Dr Williams. Thank you. That is the end of Part 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers.</p>"
