            "script": "<p>Part 2 You will hear a guide talking to a group of visitors to Pioneer Historical Village. First you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14. Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 14.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(6:07 - 6:24)</span> Hello, everyone, and welcome to Pioneer Historical Village. It's an outdoor park recreated to look just like a village from the mid-19th century. Visitors can wander through the village, look inside the houses, and get a feel for what life was like back then.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(6:25 - 6:46)</span> The village has grown somewhat since it was first opened to the public, going back a bit, in 1962 a committee was formed in the hope of preserving some of the local history. Many years of planning and fundraising followed. Volunteers worked throughout the 1970s to restore and move buildings to the site.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(6:47 - 7:00)</span> <b data-q=\"11\">In 1980 the village welcomed its first paying visitors.</b> We'll go in now, and I'll help you purchase your tickets. Since there are 10 of you all together in this party, you qualify for the group discount.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(7:01 - 7:26)</span> The normal price of admission is $16 for adults and $12.50 for students and seniors, <b data-q=\"12\">but it'll only be $11 per person for all of you today</b>, which is quite a saving. Sometimes we have shows and demonstrations. We had hoped to show you how candles are made, but the person organising it had to cancel at the last minute.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(7:27 - 7:45)</span> You're all in for a treat of another kind though. Just outside the shop <b data-q=\"13\">we have an expert sweet maker who will be making lollipops the old-fashioned way</b>, so do go and have a look. If you want to make bread yourself, head to the bakery and have a go.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(7:45 - 8:03)</span> The space is quite small though, so no spectators please. You'll have a chance to interact with some farm animals today. We used to keep cows in the dairy, but someone had to be here twice a day to milk them, and it became difficult to manage.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(8:04 - 8:15)</span> Chickens are everywhere though, not just on the farms. <b data-q=\"14\">Every house kept a few for the eggs. You're welcome to have a look and see if you can find any to collect.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(8:17 - 8:29)</span> Goats were another favourite animal, but they cause trouble too. They're fun to feed, but they eat everything in sight. The ones that we had have moved to a nearby farm.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(8:33 - 9:19)</span> Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 15 to 20. Now listen and answer questions 15 to 20. Now, you'll probably want some time to explore the village.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(9:19 - 9:33)</span> Please open your maps and we'll have a look at the layout. We'll enter through the main gate, which is at the bottom of your map. So, if you'd like to see the schoolhouse, that's a good first stop because it's not too far from the gate.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(9:34 - 9:44)</span> If you head directly north, <b data-q=\"15\">you'll come to a beautiful old church. The schoolhouse is diagonally across from it.</b> The mill is quite interesting.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(9:44 - 9:58)</span> That's where they ground wheat to make flour for baking. To find it, go straight up from the main gate <b data-q=\"16\">until you get to the clock tower. Turn right and it's on the other side of the road, across from the pond.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(9:59 - 10:14)</span> Then, there are the stables, where the horses were kept. You may have seen the back of the stables already because they're a large building backing onto the car park. <b data-q=\"17\">If you head west past the church, it's the first building you come to.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(10:15 - 10:28)</span> Medical care was quite basic in those days. To get an idea of what it was like, you might like to visit the doctor's office. <b data-q=\"18\">Look in the middle of your maps and it's in a block of three buildings that are all close together.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(10:29 - 10:39)</span> It's one of them. There's even an old hotel. <b data-q=\"19\">Go up past the picnic area and the hotel is on the opposite side of the path, near the pond.</b></p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(10:41 - 11:03)</span> <b data-q=\"20\">Another place to see is the workshop. It's at the end of that narrow winding path just south of the picnic area</b>, which is on the right-hand side of your map, at the eastern end of the village. The village has been here since... That is the end of part two.</p>\n<p><span class=\"ts\">(11:03 - 11:24)</span> You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part two.</p>"
