                "html": "<p><b>The Patagon People of South America</b></p>\n<p><b>Clothing</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>One type of coat is culturally important.</li>\n<li>Young guanaco were used to make the coats because of their [[31]] skin.</li>\n<li>Men hunted the guanaco.</li>\n<li>Women sewed the skins together into coats.</li>\n<li>Scrapers made from [[32]] are today displayed in museums.</li>\n<li>Colours from natural [[33]] were used.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The arrival of the Spanish</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>More guanaco were caught because of the use of the [[34]] for hunting.</li>\n<li>Coats played a [[35]] role rather than just being for personal use.</li>\n<li>New materials such as [[36]] improved the scraping process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Conclusions</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Coat designs date from pre-historic times – similar designs are found inside [[37]].</li>\n<li>Coat designs may have been a link with people's [[38]].</li>\n<li>Large Patagon settlements were not built on the [[39]].</li>\n<li>Shepherds still make [[40]] using similar techniques.</li>\n</ul>"
